## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end, fault-tolerant data pipelines and reason about exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in real-time systems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing of streaming pipelines
- Idempotency and deduplication strategies
- Replay, backpressure, and regional outage resilience
- Metrics: data loss, duplicates, latency, SLAs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple dedup: track seen event_ids to skip duplicates in sinks
const seen = new Set();
function process(event){
  if(seen.has(event.event_id)) return false; // duplicate
  seen.add(event.event_id);
  // write to sink
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cross-region failover and data skew in this pipeline?
- What changes would you make if sinks cannot guarantee exactly-once delivery?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical knowledge of testing streaming systems, with emphasis on edge-case window behavior, fault tolerance, and end-to-end accuracy—skills essential for building production-grade data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Windowing semantics and latency boundaries
- Idempotent and deduplicated alerting
- End-to-end streaming integration patterns
- Backpressure handling and burst tolerance
- Observability and feature drift detection

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unit test for window aggregation correctness
async function testWindowAggregation() {
  const windowService = new WindowingService({
    windowSizeMs: 5000,
    slideIntervalMs: 1000
  });

  // Simulate late-arriving events within watermark tolerance
  await windowService.processEvents([
    { timestamp: 1000, value: 10 },
    { timestamp: 2000, value: 20 },
    { timestamp: 5500, value: 30 } // Late event within tolerance
  ]);

  const result = await windowService.getWindowResult();
  expect(result.sum).toBe(60);
  expect(result.count).toBe(3);
}

// Integration test for alert deduplication
async function testAlertDeduplication() {
  const alertService = new AlertService();

  // Simulate duplicate events from Kafka retry
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    await alertService.process({
      eventId: 'evt-123',
      anomalyScore: 0.95,
      timestamp: Date.now()
    });
  }

  const alerts = await alertService.getEmittedAlerts();
  expect(alerts.length).toBe(1); // Only one alert emitted
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design robust testing for streaming data pipelines at enterprise scale. It addresses critical challenges in data engineering: schema evolution handling, fault tolerance, timing edge cases, and maintaining development velocity.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end data validation across Kafka → Spark Structured Streaming → Snowflake pipeline
- Schema drift detection and evolution-tolerant test frameworks
- Idempotent processing, replay safety, and exactly-once semantics implementation
- Late-arriving data handling and backfill scenario testing
- CI optimization through strategic sampling, data caching, and parallel execution

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test framework for drift and replay validation
function testSchemaDriftHandling() {
  // Generate baseline schema
  const baselineSchema = generateTestSchema('v1');
  // Evolve schema with new fields
  const evolvedSchema = generateTestSchema('v2');

  // Validate pipeline handles both versions
  assert Pipeline.process(baselineSchema, evolvedSchema)
    .maintainsDataIntegrity();
}

function testIdempotentReplay() {
  const testEvents = generateKafkaEvents();
  const firstRun = pipeline.process(testEvents);
  const replayRun = pipeline.process(testEvents);

  // Verify idempotency through offset tracking
  assert deepEqual(firstRun.results, replayRun.results);
}
```

## Implementation Strategy

Synthetic Kafka topics feed controlled test datasets into Spark Structured Streaming jobs, with schema validation at each stage through automated diff checks. Idempotence testing uses offset-based deduplication verification, while late-arrival scenarios are simulated through timestamp manipulation. CI efficiency is achieved via test data caching, parallel execution, and intelligent sampling strategies that maintain coverage while respecting time constraints.

